course: chinese-street
lesson: 31
title: Olympiad: tone detectives and number puzzles ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad mixes the whole course into puzzles: tones that change words, tones that quietly change inside phrases, numbers built like Lego, days that have numbers, and twelve zodiac animals going round and round.
explain: Every puzzle has one trick you can find with what you already know: line the examples up, spot the rule, then check it against every example before you answer.
explain: Picture Ming's big sister holding up a card: «Today is xīngqīsì. What is hòutiān?» Thursday is day four, two days on is day six, so the answer is Saturday, xīngqīliù.
explain: Watch the traps: Sunday has no number, 12 is shí'èr and not èrshí, and when Ming groans «I've told you a million times» he means many times, not a number to count.
fact: Mandarin numbers are built from parts: shísān is ten-three (13), sānshí is three-tens (30), sānshísān is 33, and jiǔshíjiǔ is 99.
fact: A zero inside a hundred is said as líng: yìbǎi líng bā is 108, while yìbǎi bāshí is 180.
fact: Days are numbered from Monday: xīngqīyī (1) is Monday and xīngqīliù (6) is Saturday; Sunday is xīngqītiān or xīngqīrì and has no number.
fact: hòutiān is the day after tomorrow and qiántiān is the day before yesterday.
fact: Before a falling tone bù turns into rising bú (bú shì, bú yào, bú duì); before the other tones it stays bù (bù hǎo, bù xíng, bù chī).
fact: yī changes too: yí before a falling tone (yí ge, yíxià), yì before the other tones (yì tiān, yìbǎi), and plain yī when counting or at the end of a number like shíyī.
fact: When two dipping tones meet, the first one rises: nǐ hǎo is said ní hǎo, Lǎo Lǐ is said Láo Lǐ, and hěn hǎo (very good) is said hén hǎo.
fact: Some pairs of words differ only by tone: mǎi (buy) and mài (sell), wèn (ask) and wěn (kiss), tāng (soup) and táng (sweets), māo (cat) and máo (fur).
fact: Numbers are lucky or unlucky by sound: bā (8) rhymes with fā (get rich), jiǔ (9) sounds like long-lasting, liù (6) like going smoothly, and sì (4) sounds like sǐ (to die).
fact: The twelve zodiac animals go round in order, Rat, Ox, Tiger, Rabbit, Dragon, Snake, Horse, Goat, Monkey, Rooster, Dog, Pig, so the same animal comes back every 12 years.
fact: The Year of the Horse began at Chinese New Year on 17 February 2026, so a baby born on 10 January 2026 still belongs to the Snake year before it.
fact: Dates go month first, then day: èr yuè shí hào is 10 February and shí yuè èr hào is 2 October.
fact: For two of something you say liǎng (liǎng ge rén), but inside bigger numbers èr stays: shí'èr ge rén is 12 people and èrshí ge rén is 20 people.
exercise: Add shísān and sānshí. What is the answer in Mandarin? | sìshísān | sānshísì | sìshí | shísìsān
exercise: Odd one out by size: èrshí, sānshí, shí'èr, jiǔshí | shí'èr | èrshí | sānshí | jiǔshí
exercise: How do you say 108? | yìbǎi líng bā | yìbǎi bāshí | yī líng bā | bā yìbǎi
exercise: Today is xīngqīsì. What day is hòutiān? | xīngqīliù | xīngqīwǔ | xīngqītiān | xīngqīyī
exercise: Today is xīngqīliù. What day was qiántiān? | xīngqīsì | xīngqīwǔ | xīngqībā | xīngqīrì
exercise: In which phrase does bù turn into rising bú? | bù + yào | bù + hǎo | bù + xíng | bù + chī
exercise: hěn hǎo has two dipping tones. How is it really said? | hén hǎo | hěn háo | hèn hǎo | hēn hāo
exercise: How do you say «one day»? | yì tiān | yí tiān | yǐ tiān | yī tián
exercise: Which pair is NOT told apart only by tone? | māo and gǒu | mǎi and mài | tāng and táng | wèn and wěn
exercise: Four price tags: 88, 66, 99 and 44. Which would many families avoid? | 44 | 88 | 66 | 99
exercise: 2026 is the Year of the Horse. Which animal is 2028? | Monkey | Goat | Rooster | Snake
exercise: 2026 is a Horse year. When was the Horse year before? | 2014 | 2016 | 2020 | 2002
exercise: A baby is born on 10 January 2026. Which zodiac animal? | Snake, New Year came later | Horse, because it's 2026 | Goat, the next one | Dragon, two years back
exercise: Ming's birthday is shí yuè èr hào. When is it? | 2 October | 10 February | 12 October | 20 December
exercise: A film starts at sān diǎn bàn and lasts yí ge bàn xiǎoshí. When does it end? | wǔ diǎn | sì diǎn bàn | wǔ diǎn bàn | sì diǎn
exercise: How do you say «12 people»? | shí'èr ge rén | shí liǎng ge rén | liǎng shí ge rén | èrshí ge rén
exercise: Read room 110 digit by digit, the way numbers are read out | yāo yāo líng | yī yī líng | yìbǎi yīshí | shí shí líng
test: ★★★ Ollie from Leeds goes «mate, 33 is just sānsān innit». What is right? | sānshísān: three-tens, then three | sānsān: two threes side by side | shísānsān: ten, three, three | sānshí: thirty, that's near enough
test: ★★★ Ming groans «I've told you a million times, there's like a million tones!» How many are there really? | Four, plus the light tone | A million, just like he said | Two: one up and one down | Eight, like the lucky number
test: ★★★ Isla from Glasgow says «aye, Sunday's xīngqīqī, day seven, ken». What is wrong? | Sunday has no number: xīngqītiān | Nothing at all, she's dead right | Sunday is day one: xīngqīyī | Sunday is day zero: xīngqīlíng
test: ★★★ It's xīngqīwǔ. Ming says «see you hòutiān at Chinese school», and school is on Saturday. Is he right? | No: hòutiān is Sunday, Saturday is míngtiān | Yes: hòutiān means Saturday | Yes: hòutiān just means next week | No: hòutiān means yesterday
test: ★★★ Tyler from Texas says «no cap, yí ge and yì tiān are typos for yī». What's really going on? | yī changes its tone to suit the next syllable | They're typos, he's bang on | They are two other words for one | Southern kids just spell one differently
test: ★★★ Zoe reckons bù hǎo should be bú hǎo, «same as bú shì, innit». Why not? | bú comes only before a falling tone, and hǎo dips | bù rises before every word, so she's right | bù hǎo is rude, so it never changes | hǎo falls, so bù must stay low
test: ★★★ Kai from Auckland was born on 25 January 2017: «sweet as, I'm a Rooster, cos 2017». Chinese New Year 2017 was on 28 January. Which animal is he? | Monkey, the year before | Rooster, just as he says | Dog, the year after it | Goat, three years back
test: ★★★ Ming's granny says the dumplings took «yìbǎi nián» (a hundred years) to fold. How long did they take? | A long time; it's an exaggeration | Exactly one hundred years | A hundred minutes, to the dot | One year, she mixed up the words
test: ★★★ Riya from Mumbai wants a bun and tells the stall «wǒ mài». What should she have said? | wǒ mǎi | wǒ mài, only louder | wǒ mā, high and level | wǒ ma, light and short
test: ★★★ Ella goes «8, 9 and 6 are lucky, so 4 is luckiest, it's the smallest!» Which is right? | No: sì sounds like sǐ, to die, so many avoid it | Yes: the smallest number is always the luckiest | Yes: sì rhymes with fā, to get rich | No: 4 is lucky only at New Year
test: ★★★ Lucy hears «èr yuè shí hào» and wishes Ming happy birthday on 2 October. When should she? | 10 February | 2 October, she's right | 12 February, nearly | 20 October, close
exam: Determine the Mandarin numeral equal to the sum of shíwǔ and èrshí. | sānshíwǔ | wǔshísān | èrshíwǔ | sānshí
exam: Given that the present day is xīngqīsān, ascertain the day designated by hòutiān. | xīngqīwǔ | xīngqīsì | xīngqīliù | xīngqītiān
exam: Identify the expression in which the negative bù is pronounced with a rising tone. | bù + shì | bù + hǎo | bù + xíng | bù + chī
exam: A year of the Horse commenced in 2026. State the zodiac animal of the year commencing in 2030. | Dog | Rooster | Horse | Monkey
